      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Встали!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'On your feet!' Senya replies with a single verb form meaning 'stood still' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Салат не понравился.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He didn't like your salad.' The verb for 'was liked' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он его не пробовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He didn't try it.' Senya links 'he' directly with 'him' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Вообще-то попробовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Actually, he has.' Fedya links 'actually' directly with 'tried' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "У меня в салате ремень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There was a belt in my salad.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'at' directly with 'belt'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы просто поспорили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We just had a bet.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'we' with 'argued'. Then the guest responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Чтоб я ремень съел?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'To see if I'd eat a belt?' Notice how 'so that' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'eat'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это не я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It wasn't me.' Notice how 'this' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'I'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Жрать будешь ты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You're eating this.' the guest links 'gobble' directly with 'will' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Пока всё не съешь, не уйдёшь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You're not leaving until you eat it all.' A compact expression built around 'bye' and 'you'll leave'. And Senya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А можно хлеб?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Could I have some bread?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'may' straight to 'bread'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "На.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here you go.' the guest answers concisely with the word for 'for'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
